  "subtitle": "The home console with no joystick — every game played on telephone-style numeric keypads",
  "why_scout_cares": "The RCA Studio II is the cleanest example of a lost interaction bet: a 1977 home game console whose only controller is the console itself. Two wired ten-button telephone-style numeric keypads sit built into the machine's body — there is no joystick and no detachable controller of any kind, because RCA bet that home computing would be a button-pushing activity, not a movement one. Every game, from Bowling to the TV Casino card games, is played by pressing digits; the case molds directional arrows into the keypad bezel, and pressing two adjacent keys together yields diagonal movement. The console was obsolete at launch — monochrome 64x32 graphics in a color era, months after the Fairchild Channel F and a year before the Atari 2600 — and RCA abandoned it within a year, selling remaining stock to Radio Shack at a fire-sale price. Then the story gets strange: the same RCA 1802 'COSMAC' chip that powered the worst-selling console flew to Jupiter inside NASA's Galileo spacecraft. The Studio II is also where the industry's first female commercial game developer, Joyce Weisbecker, wrote her games.",
